  "body": {
    "id": "thm:no-dominance-upper",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "There exist universal constants \\(c_0,C_1>0\\) such that, for every\n\\(0<\\alpha\\le1/4\\),\n\\[\n M\\sqrt{\\log(1/\\alpha)}\\le c_0\\sqrt S\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n \\mathcal R_\\alpha(w,q)\n \\le C_1\\min\\{1,\\sqrt{S\\log(1/\\alpha)}\\}.\n\\]\nThe infimum defining \\(\\mathcal R_\\alpha\\) may range over every\nmeasurable interval procedure randomized by \\(R\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "alpha",
      "M",
      "S",
      "risk",
      "w",
      "q",
      "R"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:length-risk",
      "thm:finite-upper"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Put \\(x=\\log(1/\\alpha)\\) and \\(y=\\log(2/\\alpha)\\).  Since\n\\(0<\\alpha\\le1/4\\), one has \\(x\\ge\\log4\\), and hence\n\\[\n y=x+\\log2\\le\\frac32x.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\nThe corrected \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}finite\\text{-}upper}\\) supplies a\ndeterministic observed-data interval that is honest uniformly over\n\\(\\mathcal M_n(B)\\) and has pointwise length at most\n\\[\n 2\\min\\left\\{1,\\sqrt{Sy}+\\frac{My}{6}\\right\\}.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\nIt is therefore an admissible deterministic member of\n\\(\\mathfrak C_\\alpha\\).  Although\n\\(\\mathrm{def{:}length\\text{-}risk}\\) also permits procedures using\nthe independent randomizer \\(R\\), taking the infimum over the larger\nclass cannot invalidate the upper bound furnished by this one rule.\nThus (18) bounds \\(\\mathcal R_\\alpha(w,q)\\).\n\nTake \\(c_0=1\\).  Under the no-dominance hypothesis\n\\(M\\sqrt{x}\\le\\sqrt S\\), equation (17) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\sqrt{Sy}+\\frac{My}{6}\n &\\le\\sqrt{\\frac32}\\sqrt{Sx}+\\frac14Mx\\\\\n &\\le\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac32}+\\frac14\\right)\\sqrt{Sx}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\nFor every \\(K\\ge1\\) and \\(t\\ge0\\),\n\\(\\min\\{1,Kt\\}\\le K\\min\\{1,t\\}\\).  Since\n\\(K=\\sqrt{3/2}+1/4>1\\), equations (18)--(19) prove\n\\[\n \\mathcal R_\\alpha(w,q)\n \\le C_1\\min\\{1,\\sqrt{S\\log(1/\\alpha)}\\},\n \\qquad\n C_1=2\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac32}+\\frac14\\right).\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\nBoth \\(c_0\\) and \\(C_1\\) are universal, as required.",
    "justification": "This theorem records exactly the proved confidence-sensitive upper frontier under the no-dominant-block condition.",
    "gap": "A matching lower bound of linear order in \\(\\min\\{1,\\sqrt{S\\log(1/\\alpha)}\\}\\) remains the weighted-erasure interval-spread question.",
    "consumer": "The result supplies an honest attainable precision benchmark for heterogeneous school, village, and network partitions.",
